WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.800
Back to the thirsty mouse.

2
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.880
Its thirst neurons go quiet within a minute.

3
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.560
No change in the blood has been detected.

4
00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.420
So what has changed?

5
00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:19.723
Researchers recorded subfornical organ neurons in awake mice, using an optical fibre

6
00:00:19.723 --> 00:00:21.303
and a calcium indicator.

7
00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:26.387
Picture the neurons and the blood on one time axis.

8
00:00:27.247 --> 00:00:28.787
A line marks when water arrives.

9
00:00:29.817 --> 00:00:32.917
The neurons' activity starts to fall at the first lick.

10
00:00:33.777 --> 00:00:34.977
Within a minute, it is down.

11
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.920
The blood was sampled, not traced.

12
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.760
Plasma osmolality was still raised five minutes after water access.

13
00:00:43.783 --> 00:00:45.323
What could turn the neurons down?

14
00:00:46.263 --> 00:00:47.263
The sight of water?

15
00:00:47.923 --> 00:00:50.283
The licking? Or the water itself?

16
00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:59.747
Seeing water it could not reach did not lower the activity.

17
00:01:00.747 --> 00:01:02.507
Licking an empty bottle did not either.

18
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.020
So it is something that comes with fluid going in.

19
00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:10.610
Drinking concentrated saline lowers it at first, like water.

20
00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:13.550
After about a minute, the fall reverses.

21
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.720
A fast signal counts fluid going in.

22
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.920
A slower one reports what the fluid contains.

23
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.580
Next, bypass the mouth.

24
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:24.840
Put water straight into the stomach.

25
00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:29.590
In dehydrated mice, the activity falls in about two minutes.

26
00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:34.830
Separate blood measurements found no detectable change in osmolarity, so the

27
00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:35.910
response comes first.

28
00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:40.613
Hypertonic saline in the stomach raises the activity instead.

29
00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:44.787
Then researchers used light to hold the neurons active.

30
00:01:45.827 --> 00:01:49.107
Now water in the gut no longer brings its usual satiation.

31
00:01:50.517 --> 00:01:52.477
Suppose the brain read only the blood.

32
00:01:53.397 --> 00:01:55.157
How much would a thirsty animal drink?

33
00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:03.997
Too much, if it drinks faster than water is absorbed.

34
00:02:05.017 --> 00:02:08.537
Water reaches the blood gradually, so a blood-only brain keeps drinking.

35
00:02:10.417 --> 00:02:12.777
So the estimate must count what is on its way.

36
00:02:14.917 --> 00:02:16.337
The brain can also learn.

37
00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:22.137
In trained mice, vasopressin neurons dipped when a water bowl arrived, before any

38
00:02:22.137 --> 00:02:23.137
drinking.

39
00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.760
So feedforward can sense fluid going in, or predict it from a learned cue.

40
00:02:29.783 --> 00:02:34.283
Keep the limits. This is population calcium, recorded in mice.

41
00:02:35.163 --> 00:02:36.483
It is not the feeling of thirst.

42
00:02:37.533 --> 00:02:39.013
Feedback reads the blood.

43
00:02:40.013 --> 00:02:41.433
Feedforward reads what is coming.

44
00:02:42.333 --> 00:02:45.473
The estimate is the blood, corrected for what is on its way.
